  "summary": "Centralia Police Chief Andy Caldwell testified before the Washington Senate Committee on Law and Justice on SB 6002, which would regulate automated license plate readers like Flock Safety cameras. Caldwell signed in as 'other,' supporting oversight but seeking to preserve law enforcement use, while ACLU-WA and advocacy groups pushed for stricter retention, sharing, and public-records rules; police chiefs from Redmond and Pasco and WASPC also weighed in.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "I fully support the need for legislative oversight to govern law enforcement use of automated license plate readers and to clarify recent conflicting lower court decisions regarding disclosure of ALPR data,",
      "paragraph_idx": 12
    },
    {
      "quote": "There is considerable misinformation circulating about how ALPRs function and how they are utilized,",
      "paragraph_idx": 15
    },
    {
      "quote": "Washington's Constitution provides one of the strongest privacy protections in the country. ALPRs collect sensitive data at a massive scale about ordinary people that are not suspected of any wrongdoing.",
      "paragraph_idx": 20
    }
  ],
